.TH VIGILANCE 1 "" "vigilance" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vigilance \- edge framework for what a machine does when you step away
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vigilant go
.RB { open | lock | sleep | suspend }
.br
.B vigilant force
.IR state ", " "vigilant only " state
.br
.B vigilant
.RB { status | verify | hooks | report | rescue }
.br
.B vigilant plan
.RI [ edge ]
.br
.B vigilant
.RB { due " [" edge ] | enforce | audit }
.br
.B swayidle-mgr ", " idle-capture ", " lock-watch
.SH DESCRIPTION
vigilance is an edge framework. It owns a LADDER of states, the ordering and
state discipline of the transitions between them, failure reporting, and
verification. It owns no specific hardware and no specific compositor: those
are plugins.
.PP
The name is the charter. Two pillars: make sure all the things go dark when
they should, and make sure the mechanism that is supposed to do it is actually
working. The second is why vigilance SUPERVISES the stock tools rather than
trusting them or patching them.
.SH THE LADDER
One dimension, how far the machine has withdrawn. Present tense throughout, so
a state names a condition the machine is IN:
.TP
.B open
Fully here; unlocked and lit.
.TP
.B lock
Session secured; the screen is still lit.
.TP
.B sleep
Peripherals down (DDC standby, backlights 0, LEDs off).
.TP
.B suspend
Machine in S3 or hibernate.
.PP
.B go
is declarative and traverses: say where the machine should BE and every edge in
between is crossed.
.B only
crosses exactly one edge.
.B force
ignores the recorded depth, for when reality has drifted from the record.
.PP
.B "vigilant go suspend"
does NOT suspend. It fires that edge's hooks, invoked BY the unit ordered
Before=sleep.target. systemd stays the actuator; vigilant only crosses edges.
.SH EDGES
A descent edge shares its name with the state it enters
.RB ( lock ", " sleep ", " suspend );
the ascent needs its own words
.RB ( unlock ", " wake ", " resume ).
Ascent is not the descent reversed: leaving
.B sleep
the saved state is valid, so a hook restores it; leaving
.B suspend
USB has re-enumerated and the saved state is stale, so the hook re-asserts.
.SH THE THREE QUESTIONS
Supervision asks three things that differ in TENSE, and no one of them can
answer another. Each is a separate hook kind for that reason.
.TP
.B verify
NOW: does the hardware match the rung we believe we are at?
.TP
.B due
SOON: is an edge pending and past its declared deadline?
.TP
.B audit
PAST: did an event that already happened produce the edge it should have?
.PP
The third is the only one that can see an event which produced NO edge. A unit
that dies on a bad
.B ExecStart
leaves nothing in a wrong state and nothing pending, so only history shows it.
.PP
.B enforce
is the loop a timer runs: it asks
.B due
whether an edge is overdue, asks the
.I block
tier whether something is legitimately preventing it, and then alerts. It acts
only when an edge is DEMONSTRABLY overdue \(em never on a tie, so the primary
mechanism wins the normal race and vigilant stays the backstop rather than a
competitor.
.PP
An IDLE-ANCHORED deadline is reported but never enforced. A
.I due
hook may print an anchor alongside its seconds, and
.B idle
means the clock starts at the last input \(em which vigilant cannot observe on
this stack. Measured from rung entry instead, it called
.B lock
overdue on a machine being actively typed on.
The forensic tier is the right home for those edges, since
.B audit
needs no idle clock at all. It only covers them if an
.I audit.d
source for them is WIRED, though, and none ships: the one shipped source reads
logind SLEEP events. An idle-anchored edge with no audit source is watched by
nothing, and
.B due
and
.B enforce
both say so explicitly rather than implying coverage that does not exist.
.SH RESCUE
.B "vigilant rescue"
is meant for a bare, unmodified key, pressable BLIND when the screen is gone. It
does two things in this order, and the order is the point: RECORD first (by
calling
.BR report ,
so the evidence is exactly what report would have shown), then recover with
.BR "force open" .
The fault it exists for is intermittent and never happens while instrumented, so
the button IS the instrument; recovering first would burn the only evidence.
.PP
It actuates nothing itself. Anything it should drive belongs in a hook, where it
is one implementation instead of two.
.SH HOOKS
Seven kinds, in TWO SCOPES:
.TP
.I /etc/vigilance/hooks
MACHINE scope, root-owned, runs for every session including a greeter's.
Peripherals belong to the machine.
.TP
.I ~/.config/vigilance/hooks
USER scope, no privilege needed. Locking, audio and notifications belong to a
user.
.PP
A greeter session needs no configuration of its own: it simply has no user root,
so it gets the machine hooks and nothing else. The machine scope is the LOWER
layer, so it comes up first and goes down last, exactly as systemd orders units:
descent runs user 10\(en90 then machine 10\(en90, and ascent reverses both.
.SS Coverage: edges that act and cannot be checked
.B vigilant report
carries a
.B coverage
section naming every edge that has actuators and NO verify tier. It is asked
STATICALLY, for all edges, and that is what makes it able to see one the
machine is not currently on:
.B verify
and the standing recheck only ever ask about the rung we are at NOW, so an edge
with no verifier stays invisible until the machine happens to be there.
.PP
Found live on both boxes:
.B resume
fires four actuators, has no verify tier, and
.B "vigilant verify resume"
reported success. After an S3 cycle is the one moment nobody is watching, and
it is an edge with a history of failing.
.PP
A verifier in EITHER scope covers an edge, because the two scopes are one hook
set when an edge runs. Only EXECUTABLE hooks count, matching the runner
exactly: a hook that lost its mode bit in a copy never runs, and counting it
would certify an edge as checked that nothing checks. It WARNS rather than
fails, being a wiring gap and not a machine fault.
.SS plan: what runs, as whom
.B vigilant hooks
lists what is wired. It lists it for THIS session, implicitly, and in one fixed
order, and both of those silences have cost an outage.
.B vigilant plan
answers the question an integrator actually has: if this edge fires, in this
context, as that uid, what runs and in what order. It names three things a
merged listing cannot.
.TP
.B SCOPE
A hook wired in USER scope does not exist for the greeter at all. Each tier is
printed twice, once per context, and the user-only hooks are listed again under
.B "absent :"
so the shortfall is stated rather than inferred.
.TP
.B ORDER
The traversal REVERSES on an ascent, so the listing is built per edge from that
edge's own direction. A restore does not run in the order a descent did.
.TP
.B REACHABILITY
.I _hooks_in
lists a hook only if it is executable, and that test is FALSE for a uid which
cannot traverse to the target. A machine-scope hook pointing into a 0750 home
therefore does not FAIL for the greeter, it does not EXIST: no log, no alert,
no missing-hook warning, and the greeter's hook set is quietly smaller than the
wiring declares. That one is silent BY CONSTRUCTION, so it is the only thing
here that sets a non-zero exit.
.PP
Reachability is judged for
.BR VIGILANCE_GREETER_USER " (default " _greetd )
by walking EVERY component of the resolved target, because one non-traversable
directory hides what is under it as completely as a non-executable file does.
A named POSIX ACL entry granting that user traversal counts: reading the mode
bits alone would report a deliberate, correct grant as a fault. Where no such
account exists the verb says reachability was not checked rather than implying
it was clean. It runs no hook, crosses no edge and writes no log record.
.PP
vigilant hardcodes no mechanism in any kind.
.TP
.IB <edge> .d
DO it: actuators that drive the hardware.
.TP
.IB <edge> .verify.d
CHECK it: did the edge actually take effect.
.TP
.IB <edge> .report.d
TELL: announce the change outward (logind's SetLockedHint, a bar).
.TP
.IB <edge> .due.d
WHEN: when should this edge have fired? The timeout lives outside vigilant.
.TP
.IB <edge> .block.d
WHY NOT: is something legitimately preventing it? Exit 10 blocks, anything
else allows. Fails OPEN: a broken guard must never suppress a lock.
DESCENT EDGES ONLY. A block may refuse to take the machine down but never to
bring it back up: honouring one on
.BR unlock ", " wake " or " resume
would leave the machine in a dark rung with its only way out vetoed, and
disabled
.B vigilant rescue
outright. Such a hook is ignored and reported by
.B vigilant report
as a wiring error.
.TP
.I audit.d
DID IT: read an external event source and report events that SHOULD have
produced an edge. Prints
.I "<epoch> <edge> [note]"
per event; vigilant owns the reconciliation against its own log. NOT per-edge,
like
.IR alert.d :
the hook names the edge in its output, because one source reports events for
several edges.
.TP
.I alert.d
SHOUT: tell the human. Not per-edge; raised by vigilant itself.
.PP
Every hook receives the edge and the rung being left, plus
.BR VIGILANCE_EDGE ,
.BR VIGILANCE_FROM ,
.B VIGILANCE_SOURCE
(who asked),
.B VIGILANCE_STATE_DIR
and two the runner states so no hook has to restate them:
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_INTENT
.BR dark ", " lit " or " none
\(em the darkness this edge implies. A hook honouring it cannot fall through a
stale case when the ladder grows a rung.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_KIND
.BR act ", " verify ", " report ", " due ", " block " or " audit
\(em which question is being asked, so ONE plugin can serve several kinds.
.PP
vigilance ships plugins under
.I libexec/vigilance/
but enables none: which ones run is the integrator's policy.
.SS Hooks are time-bounded
Hooks run in LEXICAL order, and the lock provider is not first, so a hook that
hangs blocks every later hook on its edge \(em including the one that locks the
screen. Under
.I lock-on-sleep.service
that removes the guarantee entirely: systemd kills the unit at its 25s timeout
and nothing can veto a suspend, so the box sleeps UNLOCKED.
.PP
Each hook therefore runs under
.BR timeout (1)
with a SIGKILL backstop, defaulting to 10 seconds and set by
.BR VIGILANCE_HOOK_TIMEOUT
.RI ( 0
disables it). A hook killed this way is logged as
.B HOOK TIMED OUT
and counts as a failure. The same bound applies to
.I alert.d
hooks, which are reached from a hook failure: a notifier that hangs would
otherwise block the very edge whose failure raised it.
.PP
The default is generous because it bounds a HANG, not an SLA \(em a healthy
.BR ddcutil (1)
pass over three displays takes seconds. It cannot reach a locker started with
.BR systemd\-run (1),
which is a sibling unit rather than a child; killing a foreground locker
mid\-lock would leave a frozen abandoned session lock, which is why the
architecture forbids running one as a child. If
.BR timeout (1)
is absent the bound is silently gone, so
.B vigilant report
warns.
.PP
The
.I audit.d
tier has its OWN, larger budget,
.BR VIGILANCE_AUDIT_TIMEOUT " (60s)."
An audit source reads an external event log \(em the shipped one runs
.BR journalctl (1)
over a day of records \(em and legitimately takes longer than anything on the
lock path may. Sharing the edge bound would turn a healthy slow read into
.B "audit BROKEN"
and retire the forensic tier by crying wolf. Nothing waits on it, so latency is
not the property being protected there; only the hang is.
.SS The edge budget
.B vigilant report
compares the hooks wired on the
.B lock
edge against the time
.I lock-on-sleep.service
allows, because those are two numbers in two files that must relate and nothing
else relates them.
.PP
The number that matters is not the total, it is the time before the screen is
LOCKED. Hooks run in lexical order and
.I block.d
runs before all of them, so every hook ordered ahead of the lock provider can
delay the lock itself; anything in
.IR report.d " or " verify.d
runs after the screen is already locked and risks only the unit being killed. So
.B "(block + act)"
is reported as the critical span and the total separately.
.PP
It WARNS rather than fails: what is wrong is the headroom, not the state, and a
correct machine turned red is how a report stops being read. The remedies are to
order the provider first, lower
.BR VIGILANCE_HOOK_TIMEOUT ,
or drop a hook.
.SS Monitor power, and why only standby
The
.I ddc\-monitor
plugin powers displays down over DDC/MCCS VCP feature D6. It uses ONLY
.B 02
(DPMS standby), and only on a monitor that advertises it.
.PP
D6 is not a scale of "more off", it is a set of states with different WAKE
CHANNELS. 02 is shallow: the scaler keeps listening and
.B 01
brings the panel back. 03, 04 and 05 are suspend, full off, and "turn off
display"; none of them promises the monitor is still answering I2C afterwards,
and if it is not there is NO WAY BACK over DDC.
.PP
Observed: a Dell AW2725Q advertising 01/04/05 and no 02 was driven into an off
state, stopped answering DDC entirely, and needed a power cycle at the monitor.
A panel offering no shallow standby therefore has no DDC\-recoverable off
state, and the absence of 02 is itself the signal. Such a monitor is left on
and the reason is reported; compositor\-side true black covers it, which on
OLED genuinely is off.
.PP
.B VIGILANCE_DDC_DEEP_OFF=1
permits 03/04/05 on panels lacking 02, for an operator who has VERIFIED on their
own hardware that the monitor still answers DDC when off. It never overrides 02
where that exists.
.PP
A monitor that WAS driven dark and then disappears from
.BR ddcutil (1)
detect is reported as drift rather than absence: a dark panel that stops
answering DDC is reclassified as an invalid display, which would otherwise make
it silently invisible to the ascent that should have woken it.
.SH TESTING
Two substrates run the same scenarios
.RB ( "sh test/run" " and " "sh test/vm/run" ),
and the stub one stubs ACTUATORS but never the trust root: a stubbed
.BR systemctl (1)
lies, and a lying stub is how a green test coexists with a broken box.
.PP
.B sh test/mutate
is the third tier and asks whether the guards BITE. Each record in
.I test/mutants
removes one guard and names the tests that must notice; a guard nothing kills
is decorative. The driver asserts the mutation LANDED, that the result still
PARSES, and that the named tests were green beforehand, because every failure
of this discipline here has been in the instrument rather than the subject.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Ok, including a no-op.
.TP
.B 78
NOT APPLICABLE \(em a hook saying "there is nothing here for me to do". Distinct
from 0 on purpose: exit 0 means BOTH "I did the work" and "not applicable",
so nothing downstream can tell a tier where every hook CONFIRMED from one where
every hook DECLINED. Measured consequence: on a desktop whose monitor has no
DPMS standby and which has no sysfs backlight, both screen verifiers quietly
declined and
.B "verify sleep"
reported OK \(em the screen was checked by nothing while the tier that exists to
catch that reported green.
.IP
The runner counts these apart from success and failure.
.B verify
names them ("ok (3 checked, 1 n/a: panel-backlight)") and reports an edge where
EVERY hook declined as
.BR "NOTHING CHECKED" ,
which is a FAIL: the state is unknown rather than good. An n/a raises no alert
and does not degrade a crossing.
.IP
.B vigilant verify
ITSELF exits 78 when the edge has NO verify hooks at all. That closes the same
hole one level up: the contract was honoured between a hook and the runner and
broken between the runner and its caller, so every wired hook declining was a
FAIL while none being wired was a pass. Identical state, opposite verdict.
.IP
It is load-bearing rather than tidy.
.I lock\-on\-sleep.service
runs
.B "vigilant verify lock"
as
.B ExecStartPost
and that is the suspend\-time lock guarantee; systemd reads the STATUS and
cannot read the sentence. With 0 there, emptying
.I lock.verify.d
made the unit report success and the box suspend with its lock verified by
nothing. 78 is distinct from failure, so a caller asking "was this verified"
learns the truth while
.B report
renders it WARN and still exits 0.
.IP
Using it is OPTIONAL. A hook that exits 0 is counted as having checked, exactly
as before, so no existing integration changes behaviour.
.TP
.B 1
Crossed, but one or more hooks failed (degraded, but it moved).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Refused: a precondition was not met and nothing happened.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR /vigilance/depth
The recorded rung, and the authoritative state. It lives in the runtime dir
because that survives suspend and hibernate but NOT a reboot, which is exactly
right: a boot means the machine is at the top of the ladder.
.IP
When
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset \(em a non\-interactive ssh, cron,
.BR su (1)
\(em vigilant looks for
.IR /run/user/ <uid>,
which logind maintains for any session of that uid, so the real record is still
found. Failing that it uses a PER\-UID directory under
.BR $TMPDIR ,
never a shared name in a world\-writable one, and
.B vigilant report
warns: nothing else writes there, so every rung would read as
.B open
regardless of where the machine actually is.
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-logind.service
The --user Session.Lock listener (install with
.BR "setup.sh service" ).
.TP
.I systemd/lock-on-sleep.service
SYSTEM suspend-lock unit (Before=sleep.target); needs root.
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-resume.service
SYSTEM resume unit (After=suspend.target); needs root.
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-enforce.timer
The supervision loop, every minute. Report-only unless
.B VIGILANCE_ENFORCE=force
is set; even then it will never force a descent into a DARK rung, because
nothing would be armed to bring the machine back.
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-audit.timer
The forensic pass, daily and
.BR Persistent= ,
so a box that was asleep at the scheduled time still runs it on waking.
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-idle.service
Keeps the idle timer alive. Placed but never enabled against a target: only the
COMPOSITOR knows when a display exists to connect to, so it starts this from its
own autostart.
.TP
.I udev/99-vigilance.rules
Grants the
.B vigilant
group write on exactly the nodes the peripheral hooks drive. Needs root.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/vigilance.log
vigilant's own record of every crossing, block and alert.
.SH THE vigilant GROUP
The peripheral hooks drive backlights and LEDs through
.BR brightnessctl ,
and those nodes are root-owned. Without
.I udev/99-vigilance.rules
and a
.B vigilant
group containing every user that runs vigilance (the greeter user included, if
greeter coverage is on), those writes are DENIED and the hooks no-op SILENTLY:
.B hook_dark
and
.B hook_lit
swallow the failure by design, so vigilant logs clean crossings while the
hardware never moves.
.PP
Deliberately not the
.B input
group, which also grants raw read on
.IR /dev/input/event* ,
i.e. every keystroke.
.B "setup.sh check"
verifies the group and the membership.
.SH WHAT CANNOT BE GUARANTEED
NOTHING CAN VETO A SUSPEND. A unit ordered
.B Before=sleep.target
delays the transition while it runs, and a delay inhibitor delays it too
.RB ( InhibitDelayMaxSec ),
but when either one fails or times out, logind proceeds. There is no supported
way to refuse a suspend outright, and a suite that could refuse one could strand
a laptop on a critical battery.
.PP
So the suspend lock's goal is not prevention: it is that sleeping unlocked
becomes LOUD and RECORDED instead of a green light.
.I lock-on-sleep.service
runs
.B "vigilant verify lock"
after crossing, so that "the unit succeeded" is a claim about the SESSION rather
than merely about the hooks, and the
.I audit
tier reconciles it afterwards either way.
.SH RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES
.BR swaylock ", " swayidle ", " wlopm ", " ddcutil ", " brightnessctl ,
each used only by a plugin and each degrading gracefully when absent. Run
.B "setup.sh check"
to see which are present and what each one's absence costs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR swaylock (1),
.BR swayidle (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
